const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"gentry","name":"Kenneth L. Gentry Jr.","dates":"1950–","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Reformed Presbyterian minister and author","bio":"Gentry is the leading contemporary scholarly advocate of postmillennialism joined to partial (orthodox) preterism. His doctoral work, published as Before Jerusalem Fell, argues at length that Revelation was written under Nero before AD 70, and he reads most of Revelation's judgments as fulfilled in the Jewish War. He Shall Have Dominion is his systematic defense of postmillennial optimism grounded in the covenants, the Great Commission and Christ's present reign. He has represented postmillennialism and preterism in several multi-view volumes and debated Thomas Ice in print, while also writing against full preterism, which he regards as outside creedal orthodoxy.","works":["Before Jerusalem Fell (1989)","The Beast of Revelation (1989)","He Shall Have Dominion (1992)","The Great Tribulation: Past or Future? (with Thomas Ice, 1999)","Navigating the Book of Revelation (2010)"],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","gentry-ice-great-tribulation","bock-three-views","pate-four-views-revelation"]},{"id":"bahnsen","name":"Greg L. Bahnsen","dates":"1948–1995","positions":["postmil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Orthodox Presbyterian; Van Tillian apologist and theonomist","bio":"Bahnsen was a philosopher, apologist and Orthodox Presbyterian minister best known for presuppositional apologetics and for theonomy, the view that the moral and judicial laws of the Old Testament remain normative. He was a convinced postmillennialist who taught that the gospel, working through the church and the means of grace, will bring the great majority of the world to faith before Christ returns. His lectures on the subject were published posthumously as Victory in Jesus. With Kenneth Gentry he wrote House Divided, a sustained reply to dispensational critics of Reconstructionist postmillennialism.","works":["Theonomy in Christian Ethics (1977)","House Divided (with Kenneth Gentry, 1989)","Victory in Jesus (1999)"],"sources":["bahnsen-victory-in-jesus","bahnsen-gentry-house-divided"]},{"id":"boettner","name":"Loraine Boettner","dates":"1901–1990","positions":["postmil"],"tradition":"Presbyterian (Princeton-trained) lay theologian","bio":"Boettner, trained under the Old Princeton theologians, wrote the mid-twentieth-century standard defense of postmillennialism at a time when the view had largely fallen out of favour. The Millennium surveys the three millennial positions and argues that the world will be Christianized through the ordinary preaching of the gospel, leading to a long era of righteousness and peace before Christ returns. He represented postmillennialism in Robert Clouse's The Meaning of the Millennium (1977). He is also known for The Reformed Doctrine of Predestination.","works":["The Reformed Doctrine of Predestination (1932)","The Millennium (1957)","Postmillennialism, in The Meaning of the Millennium (1977)"],"sources":["boettner-millennium","clouse-meaning-millennium"]},{"id":"warfield","name":"B. B. Warfield","dates":"1851–1921","positions":["postmil","idealism"],"tradition":"Old Princeton Presbyterian","bio":"Benjamin Breckinridge Warfield was the leading Princeton theologian of his generation. His essay The Millennium and the Apocalypse reads Revelation 20:1-10 not as a future earthly era but as a picture of the blessedness of departed saints reigning with Christ in heaven during the present age, an exegesis many classify as amillennial or idealist. Yet his broader eschatology was strongly optimistic: he expected the gospel to win the world before the end, and he argued that the vast majority of humanity would finally be saved. He is therefore claimed by both postmillennialists and amillennialists, and his label is disputed.","works":["The Millennium and the Apocalypse (1904)","Are They Few That Be Saved? (1918)","The Plan of Salvation (1915)"],"sources":["warfield-millennium-apocalypse"]},{"id":"iain-murray","name":"Iain H. Murray","dates":"1931–","positions":["postmil"],"tradition":"Reformed evangelical; co-founder of the Banner of Truth Trust","bio":"Murray is a British minister, biographer and historian, co-founder of the Banner of Truth Trust. The Puritan Hope traces the 'hope' of English and Scottish Puritans that the gospel would spread through the nations, that the Jewish people would be converted in large numbers (from Romans 11), and that this would bring a season of worldwide blessing. The book is widely credited with reviving interest in historic, revival-centred postmillennialism among Reformed evangelicals. His many biographies (Edwards, Lloyd-Jones, Spurgeon) often highlight this eschatology of hope.","works":["The Puritan Hope (1971)","Jonathan Edwards: A New Biography (1987)","Revival and Revivalism (1994)"],"sources":["murray-puritan-hope"]},{"id":"mathison","name":"Keith A. Mathison","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Reformed; Ligonier Ministries and Reformation Bible College","bio":"Mathison is a Reformed theologian associated with R. C. Sproul's Ligonier Ministries and Reformation Bible College. Postmillennialism: An Eschatology of Hope presents a modest, non-theonomic postmillennialism built from a survey of Old and New Testament texts on the growth of the messianic kingdom. He also edited When Shall These Things Be?, a collection of essays by Reformed scholars arguing that full ('hyper') preterism departs from biblical and creedal teaching on the resurrection and Second Coming. His Dispensationalism: Rightly Dividing the People of God? is a Reformed critique of dispensational theology.","works":["Dispensationalism: Rightly Dividing the People of God? (1995)","Postmillennialism: An Eschatology of Hope (1999)","When Shall These Things Be? (ed., 2004)","From Age to Age (2009)"],"sources":["mathison-postmillennialism","mathison-when-shall"]},{"id":"douglas-wilson","name":"Douglas Wilson","dates":"1953–","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Reformed evangelical (Communion of Reformed Evangelical Churches), Moscow, Idaho","bio":"Wilson is a pastor, author and founder of a classical-Christian education movement who is one of the most visible popular advocates of postmillennialism. He argues that Christ is reigning now and that the Great Commission will succeed in discipling the nations, transforming culture over long ages, before Christ returns. He holds a partial preterist reading of the Olivet Discourse and much of Revelation. He represented postmillennialism at the 2009 Desiring God panel moderated by John Piper, alongside Sam Storms (amillennial) and Jim Hamilton (historic premillennial).","works":["Heaven Misplaced: Christ's Kingdom on Earth (2008)","When the Man Comes Around: A Commentary on Revelation (2019)"],"sources":["wilson-heaven-misplaced","desiringgod-evening-of-eschatology"]},{"id":"demar","name":"Gary DeMar","dates":"1950–","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism"],"tradition":"Reformed evangelical; long-time president of American Vision","bio":"DeMar is a popular writer and speaker who led American Vision for decades. Last Days Madness argues that the 'last days' language, the Olivet Discourse's 'this generation' (Matthew 24:34) and much of Revelation refer to the first-century end of the old covenant order in AD 70, and that modern 'prophecy watching' misreads Scripture. He debated Dave Hunt and Thomas Ice on Christian Reconstruction (1988, 1989) and Ice on preterism and futurism (2002, 2006), and has debated Michael Brown on Israel and the church. Critics such as Ice describe his view as replacement theology; DeMar rejects that label.","works":["Last Days Madness (1991; 4th ed. 1999)","The Debate Over Christian Reconstruction (1988)","The Reduction of Christianity (with Peter Leithart, 1988)","End Times Fiction (2001)"],"sources":["demar-last-days-madness","demar-debate-christian-reconstruction","ice-on-solid-ground","lineoffire-brown-demar"]},{"id":"jonathan-edwards","name":"Jonathan Edwards","dates":"1703–1758","positions":["postmil","historicism"],"tradition":"New England Congregationalist (Reformed); theologian of the Great Awakening","bio":"Edwards, the leading theologian of the First Great Awakening, combined a historicist reading of Revelation (with the papacy as Antichrist) with a fervent postmillennial expectation. In A History of the Work of Redemption he traced God's plan through history toward a coming 'glorious' age in which the gospel, through outpourings of the Spirit and revival, would fill the earth before Christ's return. His Humble Attempt urged united prayer for such revival. He became a key source for later evangelical missionary optimism.","works":["A Humble Attempt (1747)","A History of the Work of Redemption (sermons 1739; published 1774)","Notes on the Apocalypse"],"sources":["edwards-history-redemption","murray-puritan-hope"]},{"id":"rushdoony","name":"R. J. Rushdoony","dates":"1916–2001","positions":["postmil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Orthodox Presbyterian; founder of the Chalcedon Foundation and of Christian Reconstruction","bio":"Rushdoony, the founder of the Christian Reconstruction movement, argued that God's law applies to every sphere of life and that the church's task is to bring all things under Christ's lordship. His postmillennialism, set out in Thy Kingdom Come (a study of Daniel and Revelation), expected the progressive triumph of Christ's kingdom in history through the gospel and obedience to God's law. His Institutes of Biblical Law (1973) is the movement's foundational text. Critics charge the movement with confusing church and state; proponents answer that it seeks change by regeneration and persuasion, not coercion.","works":["Thy Kingdom Come (1970)","The Institutes of Biblical Law (1973)"],"sources":["rushdoony-thy-kingdom-come"]},{"id":"hodge","name":"Charles Hodge","dates":"1797–1878","positions":["postmil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Old Princeton Presbyterian","bio":"Hodge taught at Princeton Theological Seminary for over fifty years and his three-volume Systematic Theology became a standard Reformed textbook. In its eschatology he defended the 'common church doctrine' that Christ returns once, after the gospel has spread through the world and Israel has been converted, followed by a general resurrection and judgment. He criticized premillennialism as inconsistent with the New Testament's teaching of one coming and one resurrection. He typifies nineteenth-century American Presbyterian postmillennialism.","works":["Systematic Theology (1872–73), vol. 3","Commentary on Romans (1835)"],"sources":["hodge-systematic-theology"]},{"id":"sproul","name":"R. C. Sproul","dates":"1939–2017","positions":["partial-preterism","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Reformed (PCA); founder of Ligonier Ministries","bio":"Sproul, founder of Ligonier Ministries, was one of the best-known Reformed teachers of his era. The Last Days According to Jesus defends a partial preterist reading of the Olivet Discourse and of many New Testament time-texts, arguing that taking 'this generation' seriously answers skeptics who claim Jesus wrongly predicted the end. He carefully distinguished this from full preterism and affirmed a future bodily return and resurrection. He did not firmly commit to a millennial position in that book, though he expressed sympathy for postmillennialism.","works":["The Last Days According to Jesus (1998)"],"sources":["sproul-last-days"]},{"id":"nt-wright","name":"N. T. Wright","dates":"1948–","positions":["partial-preterism","amil"],"tradition":"Anglican; former Bishop of Durham; New Testament historian","bio":"Wright is among the most influential New Testament scholars of his generation. In Jesus and the Victory of God he argues that Jesus' 'Son of Man coming on the clouds' language (Daniel 7) referred to his vindication and the judgment on Jerusalem in AD 70 rather than to the end of the space-time universe, a reading close to partial preterism. In Surprised by Hope and The Resurrection of the Son of God he strongly affirms a future bodily resurrection and new creation, with the 'second coming' texts in Paul pointing to Christ's future appearing. He does not frame his views in millennial categories; his label here is approximate and disputed.","works":["The Resurrection of the Son of God (2003)","Jesus and the Victory of God (1996)","Surprised by Hope (2008)"],"sources":["wright-jesus-victory","wright-surprised-by-hope"]},{"id":"hoekema","name":"Anthony A. Hoekema","dates":"1913–1988","positions":["amil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Christian Reformed Church; Calvin Theological Seminary","bio":"Hoekema taught systematic theology at Calvin Seminary. The Bible and the Future is a standard Reformed amillennial textbook, notable for emphasizing 'inaugurated eschatology' (the already/not yet) and the renewed earth as the final home of the redeemed. He preferred the term 'realized millennialism', reading Revelation 20 as the present reign of Christ and departed saints during the church age. He represented amillennialism in Clouse's The Meaning of the Millennium (1977).","works":["The Bible and the Future (1979)","Amillennialism, in The Meaning of the Millennium (1977)","Created in God's Image (1986)"],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","clouse-meaning-millennium"]},{"id":"riddlebarger","name":"Kim Riddlebarger","dates":"1954–","positions":["amil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Reformed (United Reformed Churches); pastor in Anaheim and White Horse Inn co-host","bio":"Riddlebarger, a former dispensationalist, wrote A Case for Amillennialism as an accessible defense of the view he calls the historic Protestant understanding of the millennial age. He stresses the 'two-age' structure of New Testament eschatology (this age and the age to come), Christ's present reign, and a single return bringing resurrection and judgment. The Man of Sin traces the development of the Antichrist doctrine. He blogs and teaches widely through his Riddleblog and White Horse Inn.","works":["A Case for Amillennialism (2003; expanded 2013)","The Man of Sin: Uncovering the Truth about the Antichrist (2006)"],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil"]},{"id":"storms","name":"Sam Storms","dates":"1951–","positions":["amil"],"tradition":"Reformed charismatic (Baptist); pastor and author","bio":"Storms, a former dispensationalist, set out his mature view in Kingdom Come: The Amillennial Alternative, one of the most thorough recent defenses of amillennialism. He works through Daniel's seventy weeks, the Olivet Discourse, Romans 11, 2 Thessalonians 2 and Revelation 20, arguing for one return of Christ bringing a general resurrection and judgment. He admits sympathy for postmillennial optimism but judges it unable to account for the New Testament's emphasis on suffering through the whole age. He represented amillennialism at the 2009 Desiring God panel.","works":["Kingdom Come: The Amillennial Alternative (2013)"],"sources":["storms-kingdom-come","desiringgod-evening-of-eschatology"]},{"id":"beale","name":"G. K. Beale","dates":"1949–","positions":["amil","eclectic","idealism"],"tradition":"Reformed evangelical New Testament scholar","bio":"Gregory K. Beale's massive commentary on Revelation in the NIGTC series is perhaps the most influential scholarly amillennial treatment of the book. He calls his approach a 'redemptive-historical form of modified idealism': Revelation's symbols portray the conflict between the church and evil throughout the whole age between Christ's comings, with a final consummation. He emphasizes Revelation's pervasive Old Testament allusions and argues for the later date (c. AD 95). His later work develops a New Testament biblical theology centred on new creation.","works":["The Book of Revelation (NIGTC, 1999)","A New Testament Biblical Theology (2011)","The Temple and the Church's Mission (2004)"],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc"]},{"id":"vos","name":"Geerhardus Vos","dates":"1862–1949","positions":["amil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Dutch-American Reformed; Princeton Theological Seminary","bio":"Vos, often called the father of Reformed biblical theology, taught at Princeton from 1893 to 1932. The Pauline Eschatology argues that eschatology is not an appendix but the very framework of Paul's theology: in Christ's resurrection the 'age to come' has already broken into 'this age', while its fullness awaits his return. His two-age diagram became foundational for later amillennial and inaugurated-eschatology writers. He rejected chiliasm, reading the New Testament as teaching one return and one general resurrection.","works":["The Pauline Eschatology (1930)","Biblical Theology: Old and New Testaments (1948)"],"sources":["vos-pauline-eschatology"]},{"id":"augustine","name":"Augustine of Hippo","dates":"354–430","positions":["amil"],"tradition":"Latin Church Father; bishop of Hippo in North Africa","bio":"Augustine's City of God, Book XX, gave the Western church its standard reading of Revelation 20. Having once held a form of chiliasm, he came to read the binding of Satan as Christ's present restraint of the devil so that the nations can be evangelized, the first resurrection as spiritual regeneration, and the thousand years as the present age of the church. Building on the Donatist exegete Tyconius, this reading shaped medieval and Reformation eschatology. Amillennialists regard him as their great forebear; some postmillennialists also claim aspects of his thought.","works":["The City of God (413–426), esp. Book XX"],"sources":["augustine-city-of-god"]},{"id":"hendriksen","name":"William Hendriksen","dates":"1900–1982","positions":["amil","idealism"],"tradition":"Christian Reformed Church; New Testament commentator","bio":"Hendriksen's More Than Conquerors popularized the 'progressive parallelism' approach to Revelation. He divided the book into seven parallel sections, each spanning the period from Christ's first coming to his second, with increasing intensity toward the end. On this reading Revelation 20 recapitulates the whole church age rather than following chapter 19 chronologically. The book has been a standard amillennial handbook for decades.","works":["More Than Conquerors (1940)","The Bible on the Life Hereafter (1959)","New Testament Commentary series (Matthew, Luke, Pauline epistles)"],"sources":["hendriksen-more-than-conquerors"]},{"id":"waldron","name":"Samuel E. Waldron","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["amil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Reformed Baptist (1689 Confession); pastor and seminary professor","bio":"Waldron is a Reformed Baptist pastor-theologian known for his exposition of the 1689 Baptist Confession. The End Times Made Simple presents amillennialism through a simple framework: the return of Christ is one event, bringing one resurrection, one judgment and the end of the world. He also wrote a book-length reply to John MacArthur's claim that every self-respecting Calvinist should be a premillennialist. His approach stresses the analogy of Scripture, letting clearer New Testament texts interpret Revelation 20.","works":["The End Times Made Simple (2003)","MacArthur's Millennial Manifesto: A Friendly Response (2008)","A Modern Exposition of the 1689 Baptist Confession of Faith (1989)"],"sources":["waldron-end-times-simple"]},{"id":"dennis-johnson","name":"Dennis E. Johnson","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["amil","idealism"],"tradition":"Presbyterian (PCA); Westminster Seminary California","bio":"Johnson is professor emeritus of practical theology at Westminster Seminary California. Triumph of the Lamb is a pastoral, redemptive-historical commentary that reads Revelation's visions as symbolic portrayals of the conflict between Christ and Satan spanning the church age, with a strong focus on the comfort they gave suffering churches. He interprets Revelation 20 within the recapitulation framework common to Reformed amillennialists. His work on Christ-centred preaching complements his eschatology.","works":["Triumph of the Lamb: A Commentary on Revelation (2001)","Him We Proclaim (2007)"],"sources":["johnson-triumph-lamb"]},{"id":"ladd","name":"George Eldon Ladd","dates":"1911–1982","positions":["histpremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Baptist; Fuller Theological Seminary","bio":"Ladd was the most important twentieth-century advocate of historic (non-dispensational) premillennialism. He developed the 'already/not yet' understanding of the kingdom of God as God's dynamic reign, inaugurated in Jesus and consummated at his return. The Blessed Hope argued for a posttribulational rapture against dispensational pretribulationism, and his Revelation commentary combines a futurist reading of the consummation with a literal intermediate kingdom after Christ's return. He represented historic premillennialism in Clouse's The Meaning of the Millennium.","works":["The Blessed Hope (1956)","Jesus and the Kingdom (1964; rev. as The Presence of the Future, 1974)","A Commentary on the Revelation of John (1972)","A Theology of the New Testament (1974)"],"sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","ladd-commentary-revelation","ladd-presence-future","clouse-meaning-millennium"]},{"id":"blomberg","name":"Craig L. Blomberg","dates":"1955–","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Evangelical Baptist; Denver Seminary New Testament scholar","bio":"Blomberg is a prolific New Testament scholar best known for work on the historical reliability of the Gospels. With Sung Wook Chung he edited A Case for Historic Premillennialism, which defends a premillennial reading of Revelation 20 without the dispensational distinction between Israel and the church and with a posttribulational return of Christ. He argues that the sequence of Revelation 19 to 20 and the two resurrections of Revelation 20:4-6 are most naturally read as a future earthly kingdom.","works":["A Case for Historic Premillennialism (ed. with Sung Wook Chung, 2009)","Matthew (NAC, 1992)","The Historical Reliability of the Gospels (1987)"],"sources":["blomberg-chung-historic-premil"]},{"id":"moo","name":"Douglas J. Moo","dates":"1950–","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Evangelical; New Testament scholar (Trinity, Wheaton)","bio":"Moo is a leading evangelical New Testament scholar known especially for commentaries on Romans. He has twice argued for the posttribulational rapture in multi-view volumes: in the 1984 Zondervan book with Feinberg and Archer, and in Hultberg's Three Views on the Rapture (2010). He contends that 1 Thessalonians 4, 2 Thessalonians 2 and the Olivet Discourse describe a single public coming of Christ after the tribulation. His Romans commentary also expects a future large-scale conversion of ethnic Israel (Romans 11:26).","works":["The Epistle to the Romans (NICNT, 1996; 2nd ed. 2018)","The Case for the Posttribulation Rapture Position (1984)","A Case for the Posttribulation Rapture (2010)"],"sources":["rapture-pre-mid-post-1984","hultberg-three-views-rapture"]},{"id":"grudem","name":"Wayne Grudem","dates":"1948–","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Evangelical, Reformed Baptist-leaning; Phoenix Seminary","bio":"Grudem's Systematic Theology is one of the most widely used evangelical textbooks. In its eschatology chapters he describes all three millennial positions fairly before arguing for historic premillennialism with a posttribulational return, on the grounds that Revelation 20 follows chapter 19 sequentially and that two resurrections are distinguished. He stresses that Christ's return could be soon yet has preconditions such as the gospel's spread and the man of lawlessness. He urges charity among evangelicals on these questions.","works":["Systematic Theology (1994; 2nd ed. 2020)"],"sources":["grudem-systematic"]},{"id":"papias","name":"Papias of Hierapolis","dates":"c. 60–c. 130","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Early Church Father; bishop in Asia Minor","bio":"Papias was an early second-century bishop who collected oral traditions from those who had heard the apostles and 'the elder John'. His work survives only in fragments quoted by Irenaeus and Eusebius. He taught a future earthly kingdom of a thousand years after the resurrection, describing its extraordinary fruitfulness. Eusebius later judged him 'of very limited understanding' for taking apostolic sayings too literally, a verdict premillennialists regard as biased by Eusebius's own anti-chiliasm.","works":["Expositions of the Sayings of the Lord (fragments)"],"sources":["irenaeus-against-heresies","eusebius-church-history"]},{"id":"justin-martyr","name":"Justin Martyr","dates":"c. 100–c. 165","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Early Church Father; apologist in Rome","bio":"Justin, a philosopher converted to Christianity and later martyred in Rome, wrote the earliest extended defense of chiliasm outside Papias. In Dialogue with Trypho (chapters 80-81) he says that he and other fully orthodox Christians expect a resurrection of the flesh and a thousand years in a rebuilt Jerusalem, citing Isaiah 65 and Revelation 20. Importantly, he also acknowledges that many true Christians did not share this view. Premillennialists cite him as evidence of early chiliasm; others note his admission of diversity.","works":["First and Second Apologies","Dialogue with Trypho (c. 160)"],"sources":["justin-dialogue-trypho"]},{"id":"irenaeus","name":"Irenaeus of Lyons","dates":"c. 130–c. 202","positions":["histpremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Early Church Father; bishop of Lyons; disciple of Polycarp","bio":"Irenaeus, who knew Polycarp (a disciple of John), wrote Against Heresies to refute Gnosticism. In Book V he argues for a future earthly kingdom of the righteous after the resurrection, as the fitting reward of creation's renewal, and expects a personal Antichrist who will reign three and a half years. He also offers the earliest discussion of the number 666 and states that Revelation was seen near the end of Domitian's reign, a key witness for the late date (though its wording is debated by early-date advocates).","works":["Against Heresies (c. 180)","Demonstration of the Apostolic Preaching"],"sources":["irenaeus-against-heresies"]},{"id":"ryrie","name":"Charles C. Ryrie","dates":"1925–2016","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Dispensational evangelical; Dallas Theological Seminary","bio":"Ryrie was the most influential systematizer of classical (revised) dispensationalism in the mid-twentieth century. Dispensationalism Today (revised as Dispensationalism) named the 'sine qua non' of the system: a consistent distinction between Israel and the church, consistently literal (normal) hermeneutics, and the glory of God as the unifying purpose of history. The Ryrie Study Bible spread these views widely. He defended a pretribulational rapture and a future earthly millennium in which God's promises to national Israel are fulfilled.","works":["Dispensationalism Today (1965; rev. as Dispensationalism, 1995)","The Ryrie Study Bible (1978)","Basic Theology (1986)"],"sources":["ryrie-dispensationalism"]},{"id":"walvoord","name":"John F. Walvoord","dates":"1910–2002","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Dispensational evangelical; president of Dallas Theological Seminary","bio":"Walvoord led Dallas Theological Seminary from 1952 to 1986 and was among the most prolific dispensational writers on prophecy. The Millennial Kingdom defended premillennialism against amillennialism and postmillennialism, and The Rapture Question argued for a pretribulational rapture. His commentaries on Daniel and Revelation read the prophetic portions futuristically, with Daniel's seventieth week as a future seven-year tribulation. He was a central figure in popularizing dispensational eschatology in American evangelicalism.","works":["The Rapture Question (1957)","The Millennial Kingdom (1959)","The Revelation of Jesus Christ (1966)","Daniel: The Key to Prophetic Revelation (1971)"],"sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom"]},{"id":"pentecost","name":"J. Dwight Pentecost","dates":"1915–2014","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Dispensational evangelical; Dallas Theological Seminary","bio":"Pentecost taught at Dallas Seminary for decades. Things to Come is a comprehensive textbook of dispensational eschatology covering hermeneutics, the biblical covenants, the rapture, the tribulation, the millennium and the eternal state. It argues that the unconditional Abrahamic, Davidic and New covenants require a literal future fulfilment for national Israel. It remains a standard reference for classical dispensational prophecy.","works":["Things to Come (1958)","Thy Kingdom Come (1990)"],"sources":["pentecost-things-to-come"]},{"id":"ice","name":"Thomas Ice","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Dispensational Baptist; co-founder of the Pre-Trib Research Center","bio":"Ice co-founded the Pre-Trib Research Center with Tim LaHaye and has been its leading scholarly voice. A former theonomist, he became one of the most active critics of Reconstructionist postmillennialism and of preterism. He debated Gentry in The Great Tribulation: Past or Future? (1999), debated Gary North, Gary DeMar and Ken Gentry with Dave Hunt (1988, 1989), and debated DeMar on preterism at Biola (2002) and again in 2006. With Mark Hitchcock he answered Hank Hanegraaff's The Apocalypse Code.","works":["Dominion Theology: Blessing or Curse? (with H. Wayne House, 1988)","The Great Tribulation: Past or Future? (with Kenneth Gentry, 1999)","The End Times Controversy (ed. with Tim LaHaye, 2003)","Breaking the Apocalypse Code (with Mark Hitchcock)"],"sources":["gentry-ice-great-tribulation","house-ice-dominion-theology","ice-back-to-the-future","ice-on-solid-ground"]},{"id":"darby","name":"John Nelson Darby","dates":"1800–1882","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Plymouth Brethren; former Church of Ireland clergyman","bio":"Darby, a leader of the early Plymouth Brethren, is regarded as the founder of dispensationalism. From around the early 1830s he taught a sharp distinction between Israel (God's earthly people) and the church (his heavenly people), and a secret pretribulational rapture removing the church before the prophetic clock resumes with Daniel's seventieth week. His extensive travels in Britain, Europe and North America spread these views, which shaped the Bible conference movement and the Scofield Reference Bible. The precise origins of the pretribulational rapture idea remain debated.","works":["The Collected Writings of J. N. Darby (34 vols)","Synopsis of the Books of the Bible"],"sources":["darby-collected-writings","ryrie-dispensationalism"]},{"id":"scofield","name":"C. I. Scofield","dates":"1843–1921","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Congregationalist pastor and Bible teacher","bio":"Cyrus Ingerson Scofield edited the Scofield Reference Bible (Oxford University Press, 1909; revised 1917), whose notes set out seven dispensations, the Israel-church distinction, a pretribulational rapture and a future millennial kingdom. It became the most influential vehicle of dispensational theology in the English-speaking world for much of the twentieth century. His Rightly Dividing the Word of Truth summarized the system's hermeneutic.","works":["Rightly Dividing the Word of Truth (1888)","The Scofield Reference Bible (1909; rev. 1917)"],"sources":["scofield-reference-bible"]},{"id":"bock","name":"Darrell L. Bock","dates":"1953–","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Progressive dispensationalist; Dallas Theological Seminary","bio":"Bock is a New Testament scholar and, with Craig Blaising, a principal architect of progressive dispensationalism. That view holds that the Davidic kingdom has been inaugurated through Christ's resurrection and enthronement (Acts 2) while its full, earthly and national fulfilment for Israel awaits the millennium. He edited Three Views on the Millennium and Beyond (1999) and represented progressive dispensationalism in Covenantal and Dispensational Theologies (2022).","works":["Progressive Dispensationalism (with Craig Blaising, 1993)","Three Views on the Millennium and Beyond (ed., 1999)","Luke (BECNT, 1994–96)"],"sources":["blaising-bock-progressive","bock-three-views","parker-lucas-covenantal-dispensational"]},{"id":"blaising","name":"Craig A. Blaising","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Progressive dispensationalist; Southwestern Baptist Theological Seminary","bio":"Blaising co-authored Progressive Dispensationalism with Darrell Bock and wrote the premillennial essay in Three Views on the Millennium and Beyond. He argues that a holistic, 'new creation' eschatology requires the renewal of nations, land and Israel, not only individual salvation. In Hultberg's Three Views on the Rapture he defended the pretribulational position by linking the rapture to the Day of the Lord coming as a thief. He has also traced the history of premillennialism in the early church.","works":["Progressive Dispensationalism (with Darrell Bock, 1993)","Premillennialism, in Three Views on the Millennium and Beyond (1999)","A Case for the Pretribulation Rapture (2010)"],"sources":["blaising-bock-progressive","bock-three-views","hultberg-three-views-rapture"]},{"id":"saucy","name":"Robert L. Saucy","dates":"1930–2015","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism"],"tradition":"Progressive dispensationalist; Talbot School of Theology","bio":"Saucy taught theology at Talbot for over five decades. The Case for Progressive Dispensationalism argues for more continuity between Israel and the church than classical dispensationalism allowed, while maintaining a future for national Israel. He represented progressive dispensationalism in Perspectives on Israel and the Church: 4 Views (2015).","works":["The Case for Progressive Dispensationalism (1993)"],"sources":["saucy-case-progressive","brand-perspectives-israel-church"]},{"id":"vlach","name":"Michael J. Vlach","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"tradition":"Dispensational; The Master's Seminary","bio":"Vlach is a leading contemporary dispensational theologian and critic of supersessionism. Has the Church Replaced Israel? examines the historical and theological case for 'replacement' or 'fulfillment' theology and argues that the New Testament affirms a future restoration of national Israel. He represented the traditional dispensational reading of Romans 9-11 in Three Views on Israel and the Church. His later work presents a 'new creation' model of eschatology in which nations and Israel have tangible roles.","works":["Has the Church Replaced Israel? (2010)","Dispensationalism: Essential Beliefs and Common Myths (2008)","He Will Reign Forever (2017)"],"sources":["vlach-has-church-replaced-israel","compton-naselli-three-views-israel"]},{"id":"barnes","name":"Albert Barnes","dates":"1798–1870","positions":["historicism","postmil"],"tradition":"New School Presbyterian; Philadelphia pastor","bio":"Barnes was a widely read American Presbyterian commentator whose Notes covered the whole Bible. His Notes on Revelation follow the historicist scheme, reading the seals, trumpets and vials as successive events in church and world history, including the fall of Rome, the rise of Islam and the papacy. Like many nineteenth-century evangelicals he combined this with an optimistic expectation of the gospel's eventual worldwide triumph.","works":["Notes on the New Testament (1832–1853)","Notes on the Book of Revelation (1852)"],"sources":["barnes-notes-revelation"]},{"id":"elliott","name":"Edward Bishop Elliott","dates":"1793–1875","positions":["historicism","histpremil"],"tradition":"Church of England (evangelical) clergyman","bio":"Elliott's four-volume Horae Apocalypticae is the most elaborate historicist commentary on Revelation ever written. Using the day-year principle, he correlated each vision with events from the first century to his own day, identifying the papacy as the Beast and Antichrist. He was premillennial, expecting Christ's return before the millennium. His work was the standard reference for nineteenth-century British historicists.","works":["Horae Apocalypticae (1844; 5th ed. 1862)"],"sources":["elliott-horae"]},{"id":"max-king","name":"Max R. King","dates":"1930–2023","positions":["full-preterism"],"tradition":"Churches of Christ minister; later founder of 'transmillennialism'","bio":"King, a Churches of Christ minister, developed what he called 'covenant eschatology', the main modern form of full preterism. The Spirit of Prophecy (1971) and The Cross and the Parousia (1987) argue that all biblical prophecy, including the Second Coming, resurrection and judgment, was fulfilled in the transition from the old covenant to the new, culminating in AD 70, with the 'resurrection' understood corporately. Critics, including Reformed and Churches of Christ writers, regard this as a denial of the creedal future bodily resurrection; proponents reply that the creeds must be tested by Scripture's time-statements.","works":["The Spirit of Prophecy (1971)","The Cross and the Parousia of Christ (1987)"],"sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","mathison-when-shall"]},{"id":"don-preston","name":"Don K. Preston","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["full-preterism"],"tradition":"Covenant Eschatology; formerly Churches of Christ","bio":"Preston is one of the most prolific living advocates of full preterism ('covenant eschatology'), author of many books and a frequent formal debater. He argues that the Old Testament promises of resurrection and kingdom belong to Israel and were fulfilled when the old covenant age ended in AD 70, so that the Second Coming and resurrection are past. His opponents have included Thomas Ice and Michael Brown, the latter in a debate on Romans 11. Critics regard full preterism as outside creedal orthodoxy because it denies a future bodily return and resurrection.","works":["Who Is This Babylon?","We Shall Meet Him in the Air (2010)"],"sources":["preston-we-shall-meet","lineoffire-brown-preston"]},{"id":"gregg","name":"Steve Gregg","dates":"1953–","positions":["eclectic","partial-preterism","amil"],"tradition":"Non-denominational evangelical Bible teacher and radio host","bio":"Gregg is a Bible teacher and host of The Narrow Path radio programme. Revelation: Four Views sets the preterist, historicist, futurist and spiritual (idealist) interpretations side by side in parallel columns verse by verse, without arguing for one, and has become a standard reference for comparing approaches. In his own teaching he leans toward a partial preterist reading of much of Revelation and an amillennial view of chapter 20.","works":["Revelation: Four Views (1997; revised 2013)","All You Want to Know About Hell (2013)"],"sources":["gregg-revelation-four-views"]},{"id":"rosenthal","name":"Marvin Rosenthal","dates":"1935–2022","positions":["disppremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Baptist; Jewish-Christian ministry leader (Zion's Hope)","bio":"Rosenthal, a former pretribulationist and director of the Friends of Israel ministry, popularized the 'pre-wrath' rapture position in The Pre-Wrath Rapture of the Church (1990), building on work by Robert Van Kampen. He argued that the church will face the Antichrist's persecution during the 'great tribulation' in the second half of Daniel's seventieth week but will be raptured before the Day of the Lord's wrath, signalled by the sixth seal's cosmic disturbances. He later founded Zion's Hope and the Holy Land Experience in Orlando.","works":["The Pre-Wrath Rapture of the Church (1990)"],"sources":["rosenthal-prewrath"]},{"id":"kurschner","name":"Alan E. Kurschner","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["futurism","histpremil"],"tradition":"Evangelical; prewrath advocate (Eschatos Ministries)","bio":"Kurschner is a leading contemporary teacher of the prewrath rapture view. Antichrist Before the Day of the Lord argues from the Olivet Discourse, 2 Thessalonians 2 and Revelation 6 that the church will see the Antichrist's revelation and persecution, with the rapture occurring when the great tribulation is cut short and the Day of the Lord's wrath begins. He writes from a futurist premillennial framework that does not depend on classical dispensationalism.","works":["Antichrist Before the Day of the Lord (2013)"],"sources":["kurschner-antichrist-before"]},{"id":"gundry","name":"Robert H. Gundry","dates":"1932–","positions":["histpremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Evangelical; Westmont College New Testament scholar","bio":"Gundry, a student of F. F. Bruce, taught New Testament at Westmont College. The Church and the Tribulation (1973) is one of the most rigorous exegetical cases for a posttribulational rapture, arguing that the church will pass through the tribulation and meet the Lord at his single, visible coming. First the Antichrist (1997) continued the argument against pretribulationism.","works":["The Church and the Tribulation (1973)","First the Antichrist (1997)","Matthew: A Commentary on His Literary and Theological Art (1982)"],"sources":["gundry-church-tribulation"]},{"id":"archer","name":"Gleason L. Archer Jr.","dates":"1916–2004","positions":["disppremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Evangelical Old Testament scholar; Trinity Evangelical Divinity School","bio":"Archer was a distinguished Old Testament scholar and linguist. In the 1984 multi-view volume The Rapture: Pre-, Mid-, or Post-Tribulational? he argued for a midtribulational rapture, placing the church's removal at the midpoint of Daniel's seventieth week before the outpouring of divine wrath. He is also known for his Survey of Old Testament Introduction and Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties.","works":["A Survey of Old Testament Introduction (1964)","The Case for the Mid-Seventieth-Week Rapture Position (1984)","Encyclopedia of Bible Difficulties (1982)"],"sources":["rapture-pre-mid-post-1984"]},{"id":"peter-gentry","name":"Peter J. Gentry","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["progressive-covenantalism"],"tradition":"Reformed Baptist; Southern Baptist Theological Seminary Old Testament scholar","bio":"Peter Gentry is an Old Testament and Septuagint scholar who, with Stephen Wellum, wrote Kingdom through Covenant. Gentry contributed the exegetical analysis of the biblical covenants (Noahic, Abrahamic, Mosaic, Davidic and New), arguing that they progressively unfold God's kingdom and find their fulfilment in Christ. The resulting 'progressive covenantalism' stands between covenant theology and dispensationalism. He also wrote on Daniel's seventy weeks, reading them as fulfilled in Christ.","works":["Kingdom through Covenant (with Stephen Wellum, 2012; 2nd ed. 2018)","God's Kingdom through God's Covenants (with Wellum, 2015)"],"sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant"]},{"id":"wellum","name":"Stephen J. Wellum","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["progressive-covenantalism"],"tradition":"Reformed Baptist; Southern Baptist Theological Seminary systematic theologian","bio":"Wellum co-authored Kingdom through Covenant and is the principal theological voice of progressive covenantalism. He argues that the covenants must be read along the Bible's own storyline, with typology escalating to fulfilment in Christ, so that Israel's land and nationhood find their telos in Christ and the new creation. Against covenant theology he denies a single 'covenant of grace' administered through the Mosaic covenant; against dispensationalism he denies a separate future programme for national Israel. He represented the view in Covenantal and Dispensational Theologies (2022).","works":["Kingdom through Covenant (with Peter Gentry, 2012)","Progressive Covenantalism (ed. with Brent Parker, 2016)","God the Son Incarnate (2016)"],"sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant","parker-lucas-covenantal-dispensational"]},{"id":"keener","name":"Craig S. Keener","dates":"1960–","positions":["histpremil","eclectic"],"tradition":"Charismatic evangelical; Asbury Theological Seminary New Testament scholar","bio":"Keener is a prolific New Testament scholar specializing in the cultural background of the New Testament. His Revelation commentary takes an eclectic approach, recognizing first-century referents while applying the visions to the whole church age and the end. With Michael Brown he co-wrote Not Afraid of the Antichrist (2019), arguing that believers should expect to endure tribulation and that Christ's return is a single posttribulational event.","works":["Revelation (NIV Application Commentary, 2000)","The IVP Bible Background Commentary: New Testament (1993)","Not Afraid of the Antichrist (with Michael Brown, 2019)"],"sources":["keener-revelation-nivac","brown-keener-not-afraid"]},{"id":"bauckham","name":"Richard Bauckham","dates":"1946–","positions":["eclectic"],"tradition":"Anglican; New Testament scholar (St Andrews, Cambridge)","bio":"Bauckham is a leading British New Testament scholar. The Theology of the Book of Revelation and The Climax of Prophecy read Revelation as a Christian prophetic-apocalyptic critique of Roman imperial power, with a vision of the conversion of the nations through the church's faithful witness. He does not fit neatly into the four classic interpretive schools; he treats Revelation's first-century setting seriously while affirming a future consummation. He has also written on Jude, 2 Peter and the delay of the parousia.","works":["Jude, 2 Peter (WBC, 1983)","The Theology of the Book of Revelation (1993)","The Climax of Prophecy (1993)"],"sources":["bauckham-theology-revelation"]},{"id":"john-owen","name":"John Owen","dates":"1616–1683","positions":["postmil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"English Puritan (Congregationalist)","bio":"Owen was the most eminent Puritan theologian. His sermons and writings express the Puritan hope of a future spread of the gospel, the conversion of the Jews and the downfall of Antichrist (identified with the papacy) before Christ's return, placing him in the postmillennial stream traced by Iain Murray. In a 1649 sermon he read the 'heavens and earth' to be shaken (Hebrews 12; 2 Peter 3) as including the old Jewish church-state, a reading partial preterists frequently cite. His massive Hebrews commentary develops his covenant theology.","works":["The Shaking and Translating of Heaven and Earth (1649)","Exposition of the Epistle to the Hebrews (1668–84)"],"sources":["owen-shaking-heavens","murray-puritan-hope"]},{"id":"luther","name":"Martin Luther","dates":"1483–1546","positions":["historicism"],"tradition":"Lutheran Reformer","bio":"Luther at first doubted Revelation's value but in his 1530 preface read it as a prophecy of church history, identifying the papacy with Antichrist and the Turks with Gog and Magog. He expected the end to be near and rejected chiliasm; the Augsburg Confession (Article 17) condemns 'Jewish opinions' of a pre-resurrection earthly kingdom of the saints. His historicist identification of the papal Antichrist became standard in Protestant confessions and commentary for centuries.","works":["Preface to the Revelation of St. John (1522, 1530)","On the Babylonian Captivity of the Church (1520)"],"sources":["luther-preface-revelation"]},{"id":"calvin","name":"John Calvin","dates":"1509–1564","positions":["amil","covenant-theology"],"tradition":"Reformed Reformer (Geneva)","bio":"Calvin wrote commentaries on almost every book of the Bible except Revelation. In the Institutes (III.25.5) he dismissed the chiliasts' thousand-year limit on the saints' reign as a 'fiction' too childish to need refutation, arguing that the thousand years of Revelation 20 does not describe the eternal blessedness of the church. He identified the papacy with the Antichrist of 2 Thessalonians 2. Amillennialists claim him as a forebear; postmillennialists point to his optimism about the kingdom's spread in his commentaries on the Psalms and prophets.","works":["Institutes of the Christian Religion (1559)","Commentaries on Daniel and the Psalms"],"sources":["calvin-institutes"]},{"id":"isaac-newton","name":"Isaac Newton","dates":"1643–1727","positions":["historicism"],"tradition":"English natural philosopher; heterodox Protestant (anti-Trinitarian)","bio":"Newton devoted enormous effort to the interpretation of Daniel and Revelation, published posthumously as Observations upon the Prophecies of Daniel and the Apocalypse of St. John. He read the prophecies by the historicist method, applying the day-year principle and identifying the little horn with the papacy. Private papers reveal speculations that the end would not come before about 2060, which he offered to discourage date-setting rather than to fix a date. His non-Trinitarian views were not public in his lifetime.","works":["Observations upon the Prophecies of Daniel and the Apocalypse of St. John (1733)"],"sources":["newton-observations"]},{"id":"origen","name":"Origen of Alexandria","dates":"c. 185–c. 253","positions":["idealism"],"tradition":"Greek Church Father; Alexandrian theologian","bio":"Origen was the most important early proponent of allegorical and spiritual interpretation. In On First Principles (II.11) he criticized those who expect a material kingdom of eating, drinking and rebuilt Jerusalem, arguing that the promises refer to spiritual realities. His approach strongly influenced the decline of chiliasm in the Greek East and anticipates the idealist approach to Revelation. Some of his speculative views (such as the restoration of all things) were later condemned.","works":["On First Principles (c. 220–230)","Against Celsus","Commentaries on Matthew and John"],"sources":["origen-first-principles"]},{"id":"eusebius","name":"Eusebius of Caesarea","dates":"c. 260–c. 339","positions":["amil"],"tradition":"Greek Church Father; bishop and church historian","bio":"Eusebius, the 'father of church history', preserves much of our knowledge of early eschatological debate. His Ecclesiastical History quotes Papias and reports the debate over Revelation's authorship, including Dionysius of Alexandria's doubts, and records Irenaeus's statement dating Revelation to Domitian's reign. He was hostile to chiliasm and saw Constantine's Christian empire as a fulfilment of God's purposes, an outlook sometimes called an early 'realized' or imperial eschatology.","works":["Ecclesiastical History (c. 325)","Life of Constantine"],"sources":["eusebius-church-history"]},{"id":"tertullian","name":"Tertullian","dates":"c. 155–c. 220","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Latin Church Father (Carthage); later associated with Montanism","bio":"Tertullian, the first major Latin theologian, affirmed in Against Marcion (III.24) a thousand-year kingdom on earth after the resurrection in a divinely built Jerusalem, distinct from the heavenly kingdom that follows. He also wrote on the resurrection of the flesh against those who spiritualized it. His later sympathy for the Montanist movement, with its intense expectation of the New Jerusalem, made chiliasm suspect for some later writers.","works":["Against Marcion (c. 207–208)","On the Resurrection of the Flesh","Apology"],"sources":["tertullian-against-marcion"]},{"id":"jim-hamilton","name":"James M. Hamilton Jr.","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Southern Baptist; Southern Baptist Theological Seminary biblical theologian","bio":"Hamilton is a biblical theologian whose work centres on God's glory in salvation through judgment. He defended historic premillennialism at the 2009 Desiring God panel and in his preaching commentary on Revelation. With Fred Zaspel he argued in Three Views on Israel and the Church for a future mass conversion of ethnic Israel without a separate role for Israel apart from the church.","works":["God's Glory in Salvation through Judgment (2010)","Revelation: The Spirit Speaks to the Churches (2012)","With the Clouds of Heaven (2014)"],"sources":["desiringgod-evening-of-eschatology","hamilton-revelation-preaching","compton-naselli-three-views-israel"]},{"id":"ribera","name":"Francisco Ribera","dates":"1537–1591","positions":["futurism"],"tradition":"Spanish Jesuit","bio":"Ribera's commentary on Revelation (c. 1590) placed most of the book (after the opening chapters) in the future, immediately before Christ's return, with a personal Antichrist reigning three and a half years. Protestant historians often describe this as a Counter-Reformation reply to the Protestant identification of the papacy with Antichrist. Modern futurists generally acknowledge him as an early systematic futurist while noting that futurist elements go back to Irenaeus and Hippolytus.","works":["Commentary on the Apocalypse (c. 1590)"],"sources":["ribera-apocalypse-commentary"]},{"id":"alcazar","name":"Luis de Alcázar","dates":"1554–1613","positions":["partial-preterism"],"tradition":"Spanish Jesuit","bio":"Alcázar's Vestigatio Arcani Sensus in Apocalypsi (1614) is usually cited as the first systematic preterist commentary on Revelation. He read the early chapters as the church's victory over Judaism (the fall of Jerusalem) and the later chapters as its victory over pagan Rome. Protestant historians often describe his work, like Ribera's, as a Counter-Reformation alternative to the historicist papal-Antichrist reading; preterists note that preterist readings of individual texts are much older.","works":["Vestigatio Arcani Sensus in Apocalypsi (1614)"],"sources":["alcazar-vestigatio"]},{"id":"milligan","name":"William Milligan","dates":"1821–1893","positions":["idealism","amil"],"tradition":"Church of Scotland; University of Aberdeen","bio":"Milligan was a Scottish Presbyterian scholar whose lectures and Expositor's Bible volume on Revelation are classic statements of the idealist (spiritual) approach. He argued that Revelation is not a calendar of events but a symbolic portrayal of principles governing the conflict between Christ and evil throughout the church age. His approach influenced later amillennial commentators.","works":["Lectures on the Apocalypse (1886)","The Book of Revelation (Expositor's Bible, 1889)","The Resurrection of Our Lord (1881)"],"sources":["milligan-revelation"]},{"id":"tyconius","name":"Tyconius","dates":"fl. c. 370–390","positions":["idealism","amil"],"tradition":"North African Donatist lay theologian","bio":"Tyconius wrote the Book of Rules, the first Latin handbook of biblical interpretation, and a now-lost commentary on Revelation. He read the Apocalypse spiritually as a description of the church throughout the present age, including the present binding of Satan and the 'first resurrection' as spiritual renewal. Augustine adopted and transmitted many of his ideas, making Tyconius a key source for later amillennial and idealist interpretation.","works":["Book of Rules (c. 380)","Commentary on the Apocalypse (fragments)"],"sources":["tyconius-book-of-rules","augustine-city-of-god"]},{"id":"lactantius","name":"Lactantius","dates":"c. 250–c. 325","positions":["histpremil"],"tradition":"Latin Christian apologist; tutor to Constantine's son","bio":"Lactantius's Divine Institutes, Book VII, contains one of the fullest early Christian accounts of the end: the world lasting six thousand years, a final tyrant, the return of Christ, and a seventh millennium of earthly peace and abundance before the final judgment. He represents the strong chiliastic tradition in the Latin West before Tyconius and Augustine reshaped it.","works":["Divine Institutes (c. 303–311)","On the Deaths of the Persecutors"],"sources":["lactantius-divine-institutes"]},{"id":"chilton","name":"David Chilton","dates":"1951–1997","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism"],"tradition":"Reformed; Christian Reconstruction movement","bio":"Chilton was a Reconstructionist pastor whose Paradise Restored offered a popular biblical theology of dominion and The Days of Vengeance a full-length partial-preterist, postmillennial commentary on Revelation as a covenant lawsuit against apostate Jerusalem. Late in life he adopted full preterism, though he did not publish a full defense of it; his earlier works remain widely used by partial preterists.","works":["Paradise Restored (1985)","The Days of Vengeance (1987)","The Great Tribulation (1987)"],"sources":["chilton-paradise-restored","chilton-days-of-vengeance"]},{"id":"kik","name":"J. Marcellus Kik","dates":"1903–1965","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism"],"tradition":"Presbyterian pastor and editor","bio":"Kik's An Eschatology of Victory (published posthumously in 1971) combined a preterist exposition of Matthew 24 with a postmillennial reading of Revelation 20. He argued that the dividing line in Matthew 24 falls at verse 35 (or 36), with everything before it fulfilled in AD 70. His work influenced later writers such as Gentry and DeMar.","works":["Matthew Twenty-Four (1948)","Revelation Twenty (1955)","An Eschatology of Victory (1971)"],"sources":["kik-eschatology-victory"]},{"id":"macarthur","name":"John MacArthur","dates":"1939–2025","positions":["disppremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Non-denominational Reformed-leaning evangelical; Grace Community Church and The Master's Seminary","bio":"MacArthur pastored Grace Community Church in Sun Valley, California, for over five decades and led The Master's Seminary. His 2007 Shepherds' Conference address argued that consistent Calvinists should be premillennialists, since God's sovereign, unconditional election of Israel guarantees his promises to the nation. He held a pretribulational rapture and future millennium, while distancing himself from some traditional dispensational distinctives. Sam Waldron and others published replies.","works":["Christ's Prophetic Plans (ed. with Richard Mayhue, 2012)","The Second Coming (1999)","Because the Time Is Near (2007)"],"sources":["macarthur-mayhue-prophetic-plans"]},{"id":"lahaye","name":"Tim LaHaye","dates":"1926–2016","positions":["disppremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Baptist pastor; co-founder of the Pre-Trib Research Center","bio":"LaHaye was a pastor and author who, with Jerry B. Jenkins, wrote the Left Behind novels, which brought a pretribulational dispensational scenario to tens of millions of readers. He co-founded the Pre-Trib Research Center with Thomas Ice to defend futurism scholarly and co-edited The End Times Controversy against preterism. Critics such as Hank Hanegraaff targeted his approach as sensationalist; supporters credit him with renewing interest in prophecy and evangelism.","works":["Left Behind series (with Jerry B. Jenkins, 1995–2007)","The End Times Controversy (ed. with Thomas Ice, 2003)"],"sources":["lahaye-jenkins-left-behind","lahaye-ice-end-times-controversy"]},{"id":"hitchcock","name":"Mark Hitchcock","dates":"20th–21st c.","positions":["disppremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Dispensational Bible church pastor; Dallas Theological Seminary","bio":"Hitchcock is a pastor and popular dispensational writer on prophecy who wrote his doctoral dissertation defending the late (Domitianic) date of Revelation against preterist arguments. With Thomas Ice he wrote Breaking the Apocalypse Code in reply to Hank Hanegraaff. His many popular books argue for a pretribulational rapture and a future seven-year tribulation.","works":["Breaking the Apocalypse Code (with Thomas Ice)","The End: A Complete Overview of Bible Prophecy and the End of Days (2012)"],"sources":["ice-hitchcock-breaking-code"]},{"id":"hal-lindsey","name":"Hal Lindsey","dates":"1929–2024","positions":["disppremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Dispensational evangelical; Dallas Theological Seminary graduate","bio":"Lindsey's The Late Great Planet Earth (1970) became one of the best-selling non-fiction books of its decade by connecting dispensational prophecy to the Cold War, the 1948 founding of Israel and Middle East politics. It popularized the expectation that the generation that saw Israel's rebirth would see Christ's return. Scholarly dispensationalists have often distanced themselves from his date-oriented speculations; critics cite them as a cautionary example of 'newspaper exegesis'.","works":["The Late Great Planet Earth (1970)","There's a New World Coming (1973)"],"sources":["lindsey-late-great-planet"]},{"id":"hanegraaff","name":"Hank Hanegraaff","dates":"1950–","positions":["partial-preterism"],"tradition":"Evangelical apologist ('Bible Answer Man'); Eastern Orthodox since 2017","bio":"Hanegraaff, longtime president of the Christian Research Institute, wrote The Apocalypse Code (2007), proposing an 'exegetical eschatology' that reads Revelation largely as fulfilled in the judgment on Jerusalem while affirming a future bodily return and resurrection. He sharply criticized the Left Behind scenario and Christian Zionism. Thomas Ice and Mark Hitchcock answered him in Breaking the Apocalypse Code. He joined the Eastern Orthodox Church in 2017.","works":["The Apocalypse Code (2007)"],"sources":["hanegraaff-apocalypse-code","ice-review-apocalypse-code"]},{"id":"michael-brown","name":"Michael L. Brown","dates":"1955–","positions":["histpremil","futurism"],"tradition":"Messianic Jewish, charismatic evangelical; host of The Line of Fire","bio":"Brown is a Messianic Jewish scholar of Semitic languages and a radio host and debater. He holds a historic (non-dispensational) premillennial, posttribulational view, set out with Craig Keener in Not Afraid of the Antichrist. He strongly affirms a future for ethnic Israel and has debated Gary DeMar on 'replacement theology' and full preterists Don Preston and Michael Sullivan.","works":["Answering Jewish Objections to Jesus (5 vols, 2000–2010)","Not Afraid of the Antichrist (with Craig Keener, 2019)"],"sources":["brown-keener-not-afraid","lineoffire-brown-demar","lineoffire-brown-preston"]},{"id":"j-stuart-russell","name":"J. Stuart Russell","dates":"1816–1895","positions":["full-preterism"],"tradition":"English Congregationalist minister","bio":"Russell's The Parousia (1878) is the classic Victorian argument that every New Testament prediction of Christ's coming was fulfilled within the generation ending in AD 70. He worked systematically through the time-statements of the Gospels, Epistles and Revelation. The book is foundational for modern full preterism and is also widely read by partial preterists, who accept its time-text arguments but reject its conclusions about the resurrection and final judgment.","works":["The Parousia (1878; 2nd ed. 1887)"],"sources":["russell-parousia"]}]`),i=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"meaning-of-the-millennium","title":"The Meaning of the Millennium: Four Views","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Robert G. Clouse (ed.)","George Eldon Ladd","Herman A. Hoyt","Loraine Boettner","Anthony A. Hoekema"],"year":"1977","positions":["histpremil","disppremil","postmil","amil"],"summary":"The classic evangelical multi-view volume on the millennium (InterVarsity Press). Each of four scholars sets out his position on Revelation 20 and the shape of the end, and the other three respond. It defined the four-way categories most later discussions use.","keyPoints":["Ladd argues that Revelation 20 follows the Second Coming of Revelation 19 and that the two resurrections of Revelation 20:4-6 must both be bodily.","Hoyt defends dispensational premillennialism, stressing literal fulfilment of the covenants with national Israel.","Boettner argues that the gospel will Christianize the world through ordinary means before Christ returns.","Hoekema presents amillennialism as inaugurated eschatology: the millennium is the present reign of Christ and departed saints.","The responses show that hermeneutics (how literally to read prophecy and Revelation) drives most disagreements."],"sources":["clouse-meaning-millennium"],"verified":false},{"id":"three-views-millennium-beyond","title":"Three Views on the Millennium and Beyond","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Darrell L. Bock (ed.)","Craig A. Blaising","Kenneth L. Gentry Jr.","Robert B. Strimple"],"year":"1999","positions":["disppremil","postmil","amil"],"summary":"Zondervan Counterpoints volume in which a progressive dispensational premillennialist (Blaising), a postmillennialist (Gentry) and an amillennialist (Strimple) each present their case and respond to the others. It is often regarded as the most exegetically detailed of the millennial multi-view books.","keyPoints":["Blaising frames premillennialism within a holistic 'new creation' eschatology that includes nations and Israel.","Gentry grounds postmillennialism in the Abrahamic covenant, the Great Commission and the progressive growth parables of Matthew 13.","Strimple argues that the New Testament teaches one return, one resurrection and one judgment, leaving no room for an interim kingdom.","The exchange highlights disagreement over whether Old Testament prophecies of a glorious future are fulfilled before, at or after Christ's return."],"sources":["bock-three-views"],"verified":false},{"id":"four-views-book-of-revelation","title":"Four Views on the Book of Revelation","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["C. Marvin Pate (ed.)","Kenneth L. Gentry Jr.","Sam Hamstra Jr.","C. Marvin Pate","Robert L. Thomas"],"year":"1998","positions":["partial-preterism","idealism","dispensationalism","futurism"],"summary":"Zondervan Counterpoints volume on how to interpret Revelation: Gentry (preterist), Hamstra (idealist), Pate (progressive dispensationalist) and Thomas (classical dispensationalist). Each essay walks through the structure and key symbols of Revelation.","keyPoints":["Gentry argues for a pre-AD 70 date and reads the bulk of Revelation as Christ's judgment on Jerusalem.","Hamstra presents Revelation as a symbolic portrayal of timeless spiritual conflict and victory.","Pate offers an 'already/not yet' reading combining first-century fulfilment with a future consummation.","Thomas defends a consistent futurist reading of Revelation 4–22 under a literal hermeneutic.","The date of Revelation (pre-70 versus c. 95) emerges as a disputed hinge for the preterist case."],"sources":["pate-four-views-revelation"],"verified":false},{"id":"revelation-four-views-gregg","title":"Revelation: Four Views — A Parallel Commentary","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Steve Gregg"],"year":"1997 (revised 2013)","positions":["partial-preterism","historicism","futurism","idealism"],"summary":"A single-author reference work that sets historicist, preterist, futurist and spiritual (idealist) interpretations of each passage of Revelation in parallel columns, summarizing what representative commentators say without adjudicating. The revised edition expands the sources and adds discussion of full preterism.","keyPoints":["Presents each school in the words and logic of its own commentators.","Introduces the date-of-Revelation debate and the major millennial views.","Shows how the same symbol (e.g. the Beast, Babylon, the 144,000) is read differently by each school.","Widely used in churches and classrooms as a neutral starting point."],"sources":["gregg-revelation-four-views"],"verified":false},{"id":"three-views-rapture-2010","title":"Three Views on the Rapture: Pretribulation, Prewrath, or Posttribulation","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Alan Hultberg (ed.)","Craig A. Blaising","Alan Hultberg","Douglas J. Moo"],"year":"2010","positions":["disppremil","histpremil","futurism"],"summary":"Second-edition Counterpoints volume that replaces the midtribulational view with prewrath. Blaising argues for pretribulationism, Hultberg for the prewrath rapture, and Moo for posttribulationism, each with responses.","keyPoints":["Blaising grounds pretribulationism in the Day of the Lord coming as a whole, beginning with the tribulation, from which the church is exempt (1 Thessalonians 5:9).","Hultberg argues the church endures the Antichrist's persecution but is rescued before God's wrath at the sixth seal.","Moo argues the parallels between the Olivet Discourse and Paul point to a single posttribulational coming.","All three are futurist premillennialists, so the debate is intramural."],"sources":["hultberg-three-views-rapture"],"verified":false},{"id":"rapture-pre-mid-post-1984","title":"The Rapture: Pre-, Mid-, or Post-Tribulational?","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Richard R. Reiter","Paul D. Feinberg","Gleason L. Archer Jr.","Douglas J. Moo"],"year":"1984","positions":["disppremil","histpremil","futurism"],"summary":"Zondervan multi-view book (later reissued as Three Views on the Rapture) in which Feinberg defends pretribulationism, Archer midtribulationism and Moo posttribulationism, with an opening history of the debate by Reiter. A contemporary Bibliotheca Sacra review called it comprehensive, scholarly and irenic.","keyPoints":["Reiter traces the rapture debate in American evangelicalism since the Niagara Bible Conferences.","Feinberg argues the church is promised exemption from the wrath that characterizes the whole seventieth week.","Archer places the rapture at the midpoint of Daniel's seventieth week, before the great tribulation.","Moo argues for a single coming after the tribulation from Matthew 24 and 2 Thessalonians 2."],"sources":["rapture-pre-mid-post-1984"],"verified":true},{"id":"great-tribulation-past-or-future","title":"The Great Tribulation: Past or Future? Two Evangelicals Debate the Question","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Thomas Ice","Kenneth L. Gentry Jr."],"year":"1999","positions":["partial-preterism","futurism","postmil","disppremil"],"summary":"Kregel debate book in which Gentry argues that the great tribulation of Matthew 24 was fulfilled in the Jewish War of AD 66-70, and Ice argues that it is a future seven-year period before Christ's return. Each writes opening statements and rebuttals.","keyPoints":["Gentry stresses 'this generation' (Matthew 24:34) and the local, Judean focus of the warnings (Matthew 24:16-20).","Ice argues 'this generation' refers to the generation that sees the signs and that the cosmic signs and global scope demand a future fulfilment.","The two disagree over whether Luke 21:20-24 interprets the 'abomination of desolation' as the Roman armies.","Both affirm a future bodily return of Christ and reject full preterism."],"sources":["gentry-ice-great-tribulation"],"verified":false},{"id":"covenantal-dispensational-four-views","title":"Covenantal and Dispensational Theologies: Four Views on the Continuity of Scripture","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Brent E. Parker and Richard J. Lucas (eds.)","Michael S. Horton","Stephen J. Wellum","Darrell L. Bock","Mark A. Snoeberger"],"year":"2022","positions":["covenant-theology","progressive-covenantalism","dispensationalism"],"summary":"IVP Academic Spectrum volume comparing the four main evangelical frameworks for relating the testaments: covenant theology, progressive covenantalism, progressive dispensationalism and traditional dispensationalism. Each contributor argues and responds.","keyPoints":["Horton presents the covenants of redemption, works and grace as the Bible's architecture.","Wellum argues that the biblical covenants progressively reach their fulfilment in Christ and the new covenant.","Bock argues for an inaugurated Davidic kingdom with a future for national Israel.","Snoeberger defends a traditional dispensational distinction between Israel and the church.","Frameworks, not just texts, shape conclusions about Israel, the land and the millennium."],"sources":["parker-lucas-covenantal-dispensational"],"verified":true},{"id":"perspectives-israel-church","title":"Perspectives on Israel and the Church: 4 Views","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Chad O. Brand (ed.)","Robert L. Reymond","Robert L. Thomas","Robert L. Saucy","Chad O. Brand and Tom Pratt Jr."],"year":"2015","positions":["covenant-theology","dispensationalism","progressive-covenantalism"],"summary":"B&H Academic volume on the relationship of Israel and the church: traditional covenantal (Reymond), traditional dispensational (Thomas), progressive dispensational (Saucy) and progressive covenantal (Brand and Pratt), each with responses.","keyPoints":["Reymond sees the church as the continuation and fulfilment of true Israel.","Thomas argues for a strict distinction between Israel and the church under a grammatical-historical hermeneutic.","Saucy allows more continuity while affirming a future for national Israel.","Brand and Pratt read Israel's promises as fulfilled typologically in Christ and his people."],"sources":["brand-perspectives-israel-church"],"verified":true},{"id":"three-views-israel-church-romans","title":"Three Views on Israel and the Church: Perspectives on Romans 9–11","kind":"multi-view-book","participants":["Jared Compton and Andrew David Naselli (eds.)","Michael G. Vlach","Fred G. Zaspel and James M. Hamilton Jr.","Benjamin L. Merkle"],"year":"2018","positions":["dispensationalism","histpremil","amil"],"summary":"Kregel Academic volume focused on Romans 9–11 and especially the meaning of 'all Israel will be saved' (Romans 11:26).","keyPoints":["Vlach argues for a future mass conversion of ethnic Israel and a continuing national role for Israel.","Zaspel and Hamilton expect a future mass conversion but no separate role for Israel apart from the church.","Merkle argues Paul promises the salvation of an elect remnant throughout the age, not a future mass conversion.","The debate cuts across millennial lines: postmillennialists and many amillennialists also differ on Romans 11."],"sources":["compton-naselli-three-views-israel"],"verified":true},{"id":"debate-reconstruction-dallas-1988","title":"Debate on Christian Reconstruction and Dominion Theology (Dallas)","kind":"debate","participants":["Dave Hunt","Thomas Ice","Gary North","Gary DeMar"],"year":"1988","positions":["disppremil","postmil"],"summary":"Public debate on 14 April 1988 in Dallas, hosted by Dominion Press, between dispensationalists Dave Hunt and Thomas Ice and Christian Reconstructionists Gary North and Gary DeMar. It focused on postmillennial 'dominion' and the application of biblical law. DeMar's book The Debate Over Christian Reconstruction is his summary and reply.","keyPoints":["Ice challenged the exegetical basis of postmillennialism; Hunt criticized the Reconstructionist programme for society.","North and DeMar argued that Christ's present kingship and the Great Commission imply the progressive discipling of nations.","The debate was part of a wider late-1980s literary exchange (House and Ice, DeMar and Leithart, Bahnsen and Gentry).","Accounts come mainly from participants on each side; no neutral report of the outcome was found."],"sources":["ice-back-to-the-future","demar-debate-christian-reconstruction"],"verified":true},{"id":"debate-reconstruction-ankerberg-1989","title":"Second Dallas debate on Dominion Theology (John Ankerberg Show / NRB)","kind":"debate","participants":["Dave Hunt","Thomas Ice","Gary DeMar","Kenneth L. Gentry Jr."],"year":"1989","positions":["disppremil","postmil"],"summary":"According to Thomas Ice, a second debate took place in Dallas on 29 July 1989, sponsored by The John Ankerberg Show and the National Religious Broadcasters. Greg Bahnsen was scheduled but withdrew for health reasons, so DeMar joined Gentry for the Reconstructionist side.","keyPoints":["Continued the 1988 debate on postmillennialism and theonomy.","Gentry's participation linked the debate to his preterist-postmillennial exegesis.","Details come from Ice's own account; no transcript was located."],"sources":["ice-back-to-the-future"],"verified":true},{"id":"debate-ice-demar-biola-2002","title":"Preterism vs. Futurism (Biola University)","kind":"debate","participants":["Thomas Ice","Gary DeMar"],"year":"2002","positions":["futurism","partial-preterism"],"summary":"A recorded debate on 26 February 2002 at Biola University on whether the Olivet Discourse and related prophecies are past or future. It was followed by written exchanges, including DeMar's article 'On Thin Ice' and Ice's reply 'On Solid Ground'.","keyPoints":["DeMar argued from the second-person 'you' and 'this generation' that Jesus addressed his own hearers.","Ice countered that the generic 'you' (as in Deuteronomy 4 and 30) can address the nation across generations.","DeMar's critique targeted dispensational timelines, not the hope of a future Second Coming.","Each side later disputed the other's account of what had been answered."],"sources":["ice-on-solid-ground"],"verified":true},{"id":"debate-ice-demar-2006","title":"The Great Tribulation: Past or Future? (Ice vs. DeMar)","kind":"debate","participants":["Thomas Ice","Gary DeMar"],"year":"2006","positions":["futurism","partial-preterism"],"summary":"Thomas Ice reports a further debate with Gary DeMar on 26 May 2006 under the title 'The Great Tribulation: Past or Future?', revisiting the questions of his 1999 book with Kenneth Gentry.","keyPoints":["Focused on whether Matthew 24:15-31 was fulfilled in AD 70.","Same title and issues as the Ice-Gentry book (1999).","Date and title come from Ice's writings; venue and recording were not confirmed."],"sources":["ice-answers-demar"],"verified":false},{"id":"desiring-god-evening-of-eschatology","title":"An Evening of Eschatology","kind":"debate","participants":["John Piper (moderator)","Jim Hamilton","Sam Storms","Douglas Wilson"],"year":"2009","positions":["histpremil","amil","postmil"],"summary":"A two-hour moderated roundtable held 27 September 2009 at Bethlehem Baptist Church in Minneapolis, sponsored by Desiring God and Bethlehem College & Seminary. Hamilton (historic premillennial), Storms (amillennial) and Wilson (postmillennial) presented and cross-examined one another; Piper said he holds historic premillennialism and regards amillennialism as the next most plausible view.","keyPoints":["Hamilton read Revelation 19–20 sequentially, with the millennium following Christ's return.","Storms read Revelation 20 as the present reign of Christ and departed saints, recapitulating the church age.","Wilson argued that Christ reigns now and the gospel will progressively disciple the nations before his return.","A notably irenic example of all three millennial views debated by Reformed evangelicals together."],"sources":["desiringgod-evening-of-eschatology"],"verified":true},{"id":"debate-brown-demar-israel","title":"Dr. Brown Debates Gary DeMar on Israel and the Church","kind":"debate","participants":["Michael L. Brown","Gary DeMar"],"year":"undated (online by 2021)","positions":["histpremil","postmil","partial-preterism"],"summary":"Audio debate hosted by The Line of Fire on 'replacement theology', Israel's role in prophecy and God's future purposes for the Jewish people. Brown, a Messianic Jewish historic premillennialist, defended a distinct future for ethnic Israel; DeMar, a postmillennial partial preterist, argued that the church is the continuation of true Israel while rejecting the 'replacement' label.","keyPoints":["Disagreement centred on Romans 11 and the Old Testament restoration promises.","Brown stressed promised national deliverance and the embrace of the Messiah by Israel.","DeMar stressed New Testament fulfilment of Israel's hopes in Christ.","Date and full content were not confirmed."],"sources":["lineoffire-brown-demar"],"verified":true},{"id":"debate-brown-preston-romans-11","title":"Dr. Brown Debates Don Preston on Romans 11","kind":"debate","participants":["Michael L. Brown","Don K. Preston"],"year":"undated","positions":["histpremil","full-preterism"],"summary":"Line of Fire debate in which full preterist Don Preston argued that the salvation of 'all Israel' in Romans 11 was fulfilled by AD 70, and Brown argued for a still-future national turning of Israel to the Messiah tied to Christ's future return.","keyPoints":["Centred on Romans 11:25-27 and its citation of Isaiah 59.","Preston links the 'coming of the Deliverer' to the AD 70 consummation of the old covenant.","Brown affirms a future bodily return and resurrection, which Preston denies.","Date and full content were not confirmed."],"sources":["lineoffire-brown-preston"],"verified":true},{"id":"debate-brown-sullivan-full-preterism","title":"Dr. Brown Debates Michael Sullivan on Full Preterism","kind":"debate","participants":["Michael L. Brown","Michael J. Sullivan"],"year":"undated","positions":["histpremil","full-preterism"],"summary":"Line of Fire debate in which Sullivan, a 'sovereign grace' full preterist, argued that the Second Coming, resurrection and judgment were fulfilled at the end of the old covenant age in AD 70; Brown defended the historic expectation of a future bodily return and resurrection.","keyPoints":["Sullivan builds on New Testament time-statements ('at hand', 'this generation').","Brown appeals to bodily-resurrection texts such as 1 Corinthians 15 and the creedal consensus.","Illustrates how full preterism is engaged by premillennialists as well as by Reformed postmillennialists and amillennialists.","Date and full content were not confirmed."],"sources":["lineoffire-brown-sullivan"],"verified":true},{"id":"dominion-theology-house-divided","title":"Dominion Theology: Blessing or Curse? and House Divided (print exchange)","kind":"book","participants":["H. Wayne House","Thomas Ice","Greg L. Bahnsen","Kenneth L. Gentry Jr."],"year":"1988–1989","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","postmil"],"summary":"A book-length printed debate. House and Ice (Multnomah, 1988) critiqued theonomy and Reconstructionist postmillennialism from a dispensational perspective; Bahnsen and Gentry replied point by point in House Divided: The Break-Up of Dispensational Theology (1989), defending theonomic postmillennialism and criticizing dispensationalism.","keyPoints":["House and Ice argued that the Mosaic law does not bind the nations today and that the church age ends in apostasy, not triumph.","Bahnsen defended the continuing validity of God's law; Gentry defended postmillennial exegesis.","Ice had himself been a theonomist until 1986, which lent the exchange intensity.","Each side regarded the other's book as a decisive answer."],"sources":["house-ice-dominion-theology","bahnsen-gentry-house-divided"],"verified":true},{"id":"apocalypse-code-exchange","title":"The Apocalypse Code and Breaking the Apocalypse Code (print exchange)","kind":"book","participants":["Hank Hanegraaff","Thomas Ice","Mark Hitchcock"],"year":"2007–c. 2008","positions":["partial-preterism","disppremil"],"summary":"Hanegraaff's The Apocalypse Code (2007) attacked the Left Behind style of dispensational futurism and proposed an 'exegetical eschatology' reading much of Revelation as fulfilled in AD 70. Ice reviewed it critically and, with Mark Hitchcock, published Breaking the Apocalypse Code in reply.","keyPoints":["Hanegraaff argued for an early date of Revelation and against Christian Zionism.","Ice and Hitchcock argued that Hanegraaff misrepresented Tim LaHaye and abandoned a literal hermeneutic.","Both sides affirm a future bodily return and resurrection.","Publisher and year of the Ice-Hitchcock book were not confirmed online."],"sources":["hanegraaff-apocalypse-code","ice-review-apocalypse-code","ice-hitchcock-breaking-code"],"verified":true},{"id":"when-shall-these-things-be","title":"When Shall These Things Be? A Reformed Response to Hyper-Preterism","kind":"book","participants":["Keith A. Mathison (ed.)","Richard L. Pratt Jr.","Simon J. Kistemaker","Kenneth L. Gentry Jr.","Douglas Wilson","Charles E. Hill","Robert B. Strimple"],"year":"2004","positions":["partial-preterism","amil","postmil"],"summary":"A multi-author Reformed critique of full ('hyper') preterism by amillennial and postmillennial scholars, addressing creeds, history, hermeneutics, the resurrection and the Second Coming. Full preterists published replies arguing that the critics did not answer the time-texts.","keyPoints":["Argues that the creeds' affirmation of future return and resurrection reflects the church's universal reading of Scripture.","Several chapters examine the resurrection texts (1 Corinthians 15) and argue they require a future bodily resurrection.","Gentry argues that partial preterists can consistently distinguish AD 70 from the final coming.","Notable as postmillennialists and amillennialists jointly opposing full preterism."],"sources":["mathison-when-shall"],"verified":false},{"id":"he-shall-have-dominion","title":"He Shall Have Dominion: A Postmillennial Eschatology","kind":"book","participants":["Kenneth L. Gentry Jr."],"year":"1992","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism"],"summary":"Widely regarded as the most comprehensive modern defense of postmillennialism, combining biblical theology of the covenants, exegesis of Old Testament kingdom prophecies and the Great Commission, and a preterist reading of the Olivet Discourse and Revelation.","keyPoints":["Traces a theme of redemptive victory from Genesis 3:15 through the Abrahamic and Davidic covenants.","Reads Psalm 110 and 1 Corinthians 15:24-26 as Christ reigning until his enemies are subdued before his return.","Argues the kingdom grows gradually like leaven and mustard seed (Matthew 13).","Answers objections drawn from texts on apostasy and suffering."],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"verified":false},{"id":"before-jerusalem-fell","title":"Before Jerusalem Fell: Dating the Book of Revelation","kind":"book","participants":["Kenneth L. Gentry Jr."],"year":"1989","positions":["partial-preterism"],"summary":"A detailed argument for a Neronic date of Revelation (c. AD 65–66), examining external evidence (including the interpretation of Irenaeus) and internal evidence (the temple in Revelation 11, the seven kings of Revelation 17). The date is disputed; late-date scholars such as Beale and Hitchcock reply that the external evidence for c. AD 95 is strong.","keyPoints":["Argues Irenaeus's statement may refer to John, not the vision, being seen under Domitian (a disputed reading).","Reads Revelation 11:1-2 as presupposing the temple still standing.","Identifies the sixth king of Revelation 17:10 with Nero.","Links 666 to the gematria of 'Nero Caesar' in Hebrew letters."],"sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell"],"verified":false},{"id":"case-for-amillennialism","title":"A Case for Amillennialism: Understanding the End Times","kind":"book","participants":["Kim Riddlebarger"],"year":"2003 (expanded 2013)","positions":["amil"],"summary":"An accessible defense of amillennialism as the historic Protestant view, organized around the 'two-age' structure of New Testament eschatology and Christ's present reign.","keyPoints":["Argues that 'this age' and 'the age to come' leave no room for an interim millennium.","Reads Revelation 20 as recapitulating the church age.","Critiques dispensational and postmillennial readings of Daniel 9 and Romans 11.","Explains the 'already/not yet' as tension between present victory and present suffering."],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil"],"verified":false},{"id":"kingdom-come-storms","title":"Kingdom Come: The Amillennial Alternative","kind":"book","participants":["Sam Storms"],"year":"2013","positions":["amil"],"summary":"A thorough exegetical defense of amillennialism by a former dispensationalist, covering Daniel's seventy weeks, the Olivet Discourse, Romans 11, 2 Thessalonians 2 and Revelation 20, with chapters interacting with premillennialism and postmillennialism.","keyPoints":["Argues that 1 Corinthians 15:23-26 places the defeat of death at Christ's return, ruling out post-return death in a millennium.","Reads Revelation 20:1-3 as Satan restrained from deceiving the nations during the gospel age.","Accepts preterist readings of much of the Olivet Discourse.","Critiques postmillennialism for underweighting suffering in the present age."],"sources":["storms-kingdom-come"],"verified":false},{"id":"bible-and-the-future","title":"The Bible and the Future","kind":"book","participants":["Anthony A. Hoekema"],"year":"1979","positions":["amil"],"summary":"A standard Reformed textbook on eschatology covering both personal and cosmic eschatology, notable for 'inaugurated eschatology' and its emphasis on the new earth.","keyPoints":["Argues that the New Testament presents the end as already begun in Christ.","Argues the final hope is a renewed earth, not an ethereal heaven.","Prefers 'realized millennialism' as a positive label for amillennialism.","Includes critiques of dispensational premillennialism."],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future"],"verified":false},{"id":"case-historic-premillennialism","title":"A Case for Historic Premillennialism: An Alternative to 'Left Behind' Eschatology","kind":"book","participants":["Craig L. Blomberg and Sung Wook Chung (eds.)","various contributors"],"year":"2009","positions":["histpremil"],"summary":"A multi-author case for premillennialism without dispensational distinctives and with a posttribulational rapture, drawing on church history, the Old Testament, the Gospels, Paul and Revelation.","keyPoints":["Argues the early church's chiliasm was historic premillennial, not dispensational.","Reads Revelation 19–20 sequentially, with two bodily resurrections.","Rejects a pretribulational rapture in favour of a single return.","Presents the view as a middle way between dispensationalism and amillennialism."],"sources":["blomberg-chung-historic-premil"],"verified":false},{"id":"things-to-come","title":"Things to Come: A Study in Biblical Eschatology","kind":"book","participants":["J. Dwight Pentecost"],"year":"1958","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","futurism"],"summary":"The classic comprehensive textbook of classical dispensational eschatology, covering hermeneutics, the biblical covenants, the rapture, Daniel's seventieth week, the millennium and the eternal state.","keyPoints":["Argues for a consistent literal hermeneutic as the basis of premillennialism.","Treats the Abrahamic, Davidic and New covenants as unconditional promises to national Israel.","Defends a pretribulational rapture.","Describes the millennium as the fulfilment of Israel's kingdom promises."],"sources":["pentecost-things-to-come"],"verified":false},{"id":"progressive-dispensationalism-book","title":"Progressive Dispensationalism","kind":"book","participants":["Craig A. Blaising","Darrell L. Bock"],"year":"1993","positions":["dispensationalism","disppremil"],"summary":"The defining statement of progressive dispensationalism, which sees the dispensations as progressive stages in one plan and the Davidic kingdom as already inaugurated, while retaining a future for Israel and a premillennial return.","keyPoints":["Argues Christ now reigns on David's throne at the Father's right hand (Acts 2:30-36).","Sees the church as a stage in, not a parenthesis to, the kingdom programme.","Maintains a future national and territorial restoration of Israel.","Moves toward a holistic new-creation eschatology."],"sources":["blaising-bock-progressive"],"verified":false},{"id":"kingdom-through-covenant-book","title":"Kingdom through Covenant: A Biblical-Theological Understanding of the Covenants","kind":"book","participants":["Peter J. Gentry","Stephen J. Wellum"],"year":"2012 (2nd ed. 2018)","positions":["progressive-covenantalism"],"summary":"A major biblical-theological study of the covenants proposing 'progressive covenantalism' as a middle way between covenant theology and dispensationalism.","keyPoints":["Traces the covenants from creation to the new covenant as the backbone of the biblical story.","Argues typology escalates to fulfilment in Christ.","Reads Israel's land promise as fulfilled in the new creation.","Rejects both a single covenant of grace and a separate future plan for national Israel."],"sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant"],"verified":false},{"id":"the-parousia-russell","title":"The Parousia","kind":"book","participants":["J. Stuart Russell"],"year":"1878","positions":["full-preterism"],"summary":"A systematic study of every New Testament reference to Christ's coming, concluding that all were fulfilled within the apostolic generation, culminating in AD 70. It is the founding classic of full preterism and is also cited by partial preterists for its time-text analysis. Critics note that its conclusions deny the creedal future bodily resurrection and return.","keyPoints":["Treats the time-statements ('soon', 'at hand', 'this generation') as decisive.","Reads the Olivet Discourse, Thessalonians and Revelation as one prophetic horizon.","Applies the resurrection and judgment to the AD 70 transition.","Remains in print and influential among full and partial preterists."],"sources":["russell-parousia"],"verified":false},{"id":"puritan-hope","title":"The Puritan Hope: Revival and the Interpretation of Prophecy","kind":"book","participants":["Iain H. Murray"],"year":"1971","positions":["postmil"],"summary":"A historical study of the eschatological hope of the English and Scottish Puritans and their heirs, centred on the future conversion of the Jews (Romans 11) and the worldwide spread of the gospel, and its role in the modern missionary movement.","keyPoints":["Traces the hope through Owen, the Westminster divines, Edwards and the early missionaries.","Highlights Romans 11 as the key text for a future large-scale conversion of Israel.","Connects eschatology with prayer for revival.","Often credited with reviving historic postmillennialism among Reformed evangelicals."],"sources":["murray-puritan-hope"],"verified":false},{"id":"city-of-god-book-xx","title":"The City of God, Book XX","kind":"book","participants":["Augustine of Hippo"],"year":"c. 426","positions":["amil"],"summary":"Augustine's treatment of the last judgment, including his influential reading of Revelation 20 as the present age of the church, which dominated Western interpretation for a millennium.","keyPoints":["The binding of Satan is his restraint from seducing the nations, allowing the church's growth.","The first resurrection is spiritual regeneration; the second is bodily.","The thousand years signifies the whole period of the church (or a perfect number).","Augustine notes he once held a form of chiliasm before rejecting carnal versions of it."],"sources":["augustine-city-of-god"],"verified":false},{"id":"beale-revelation-commentary","title":"The Book of Revelation (New International Greek Testament Commentary)","kind":"book","participants":["G. K. Beale"],"year":"1999","positions":["amil","idealism","eclectic"],"summary":"A massive exegetical commentary arguing for a 'redemptive-historical form of modified idealism' and a late (c. AD 95) date, with extensive attention to Old Testament allusions.","keyPoints":["Revelation's visions recapitulate the age between Christ's comings.","Old Testament allusions (especially Daniel) are the key to the symbols.","Revelation 20 depicts the present reign of believers and Satan's restraint.","Defends the late date while acknowledging the strength of some early-date arguments."],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc"],"verified":false},{"id":"not-afraid-of-the-antichrist","title":"Not Afraid of the Antichrist: Why We Don't Believe in a Pre-Tribulation Rapture","kind":"book","participants":["Michael L. Brown","Craig S. Keener"],"year":"2019","positions":["histpremil","futurism"],"summary":"Two charismatic scholars argue from the New Testament that believers will face the end-time tribulation and that Christ's return is a single posttribulational event.","keyPoints":["Argues the rapture and the Second Coming are the same event (Matthew 24:29-31; 1 Thessalonians 4).","Holds that 'wrath' from which believers are spared is God's judgment, not persecution.","Encourages readiness for suffering rather than escape.","Pretribulational reviewers argue the authors misread the Day of the Lord texts."],"sources":["brown-keener-not-afraid"],"verified":true},{"id":"pre-wrath-rapture-book","title":"The Pre-Wrath Rapture of the Church","kind":"book","participants":["Marvin Rosenthal"],"year":"1990","positions":["futurism","disppremil"],"summary":"The book that popularized the prewrath view, placing the rapture after the great tribulation begins but before the Day of the Lord's wrath, within the second half of Daniel's seventieth week.","keyPoints":["Distinguishes the 'great tribulation' (man's and Satan's wrath) from the Day of the Lord (God's wrath).","Links the cosmic signs of the sixth seal (Revelation 6:12-17) with Matthew 24:29.","Argues the church sees the Antichrist (2 Thessalonians 2:1-4).","Critiqued by pretribulationists for dividing the seventieth week."],"sources":["rosenthal-prewrath"],"verified":false},{"id":"horae-apocalypticae","title":"Horae Apocalypticae","kind":"book","participants":["Edward Bishop Elliott"],"year":"1844","positions":["historicism"],"summary":"The most elaborate historicist commentary on Revelation, correlating each seal, trumpet and vial with events in church history using the day-year principle.","keyPoints":["Reads the trumpets as barbarian and Islamic invasions of the Roman world.","Identifies the papacy as the Beast and Antichrist.","Uses the day-year principle for the 1,260 days.","Shows both the learning and the difficulty of fixing prophetic dates."],"sources":["elliott-horae"],"verified":false},{"id":"last-days-according-to-jesus","title":"The Last Days According to Jesus","kind":"book","participants":["R. C. Sproul"],"year":"1998","positions":["partial-preterism"],"summary":"A popular-level defense of partial preterism that takes the time-texts of the Olivet Discourse seriously in reply to skeptics, while affirming a future return and rejecting full preterism.","keyPoints":["Uses Bertrand Russell's objection to Jesus' predictions as a starting point.","Reads 'this generation' as Jesus' contemporaries.","Distinguishes partial from full preterism.","Engages Gentry's case for an early date of Revelation."],"sources":["sproul-last-days"],"verified":false},{"id":"jesus-and-the-victory-of-god","title":"Jesus and the Victory of God","kind":"book","participants":["N. T. Wright"],"year":"1996","positions":["partial-preterism"],"summary":"A major historical study of Jesus that reads the Olivet Discourse's 'coming of the Son of Man' as Jesus' vindication and the judgment on Jerusalem, interpreted through Daniel 7 and Jewish apocalyptic imagery.","keyPoints":["Apocalyptic cosmic language describes socio-political events with theological significance.","Jesus predicted the temple's fall as divine judgment.","The 'coming' of Daniel 7 is upward to God, signifying vindication.","Wright still affirms a future bodily resurrection and new creation."],"sources":["wright-jesus-victory"],"verified":false}]`),t={scholars:e,library:i},{scholars:a,library:n}=t;export{n as l,a as s};
